<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: #ffffff; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # What Is Sustainable Fashion? – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Sustainable Fashion</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> What Is Sustainable Fashion?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Summarize complex texts for peers. Communication: Resolve conflicts in group settings.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can summarize points from a fashion article by listing keywords (e.g., "eco-friendly" and "vintage" on a board as examples of trends).
            Can resolve group disagreements on fashion ideas by guiding the discussion with simple language (e.g., "Can we choose one trend, like eco-fashion?" while showing a picture of eco-friendly materials).
            Can explain fashion terms, using visuals and simple phrases in group discussions (e.g., "Sustainable means making choices that reduce environmental impact," while holding up eco-friendly fabric samples).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 55%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a "Sustainable Fashion Word Cloud" on the board (containing words like <em>eco-friendly, vintage, recycled, ethical, environment, evolve, predict, materials</em>). Students work in pairs to quickly define 3-4 words in their own words, activating prior knowledge on the theme. For example, "What does 'eco-friendly' mean to you?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to explain a complex term concisely. "When we talk about 'sustainability' in fashion, it means making choices that reduce environmental impact and are fair to people. For example, using recycled materials instead of new ones." The teacher also models how to guide a discussion by asking, "Can we choose one trend, like eco-fashion?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I need to 'summarize' an article, do I read every single sentence?" (No, you get the main ideas.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I want to 'explain' something to a friend, do I use difficult words or simple words and examples?" (Simple words and examples.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Sustainable Fashion".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short online article (news blog excerpt) or a visually appealing infographic titled "The Future of Fashion: Innovation and Conscientious Choices." The content focuses on predictions using the future simple (e.g., "Fashion trends <em>will continue to evolve</em>," "Designers <em>will launch</em> more sustainable collections") and incorporates target vocabulary like <em>sustainable, eco-friendly, materials, prediction, industry, brands, designs</em>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Sociolinguistic: Organizing discussions about the future of fashion by focusing on how language reflects people's values and attitudes toward sustainability, and providing examples of sustainable practices and eco-friendly materials.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read/view the material individually. Then, in pairs, they answer gist questions:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the main prediction about future fashion in this article/infographic?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What are two new materials or practices mentioned that will become important?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How does the text suggest consumers' values will affect the fashion industry?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Grammar: Past continuous with present simple to compare trends (e.g., "In the early 2000s, people were wearing low-rise jeans. Today, both flared and ripped jeans are in style."). Past perfect for completed actions (e.g., "In the last decades, people had adopted more casual fashion styles, leaving the formal trends behind."). Future simple for predictions (e.g., "Fashion trends will continue to evolve."). Vocabulary: <em>fashion, trend, vintage, eco-friendly, sustainability, materials, evolve, predict, recycle</em>. Pronunciation: Stress in multisyllabic words (<em>fashionable, sustainability, eco-friendly</em>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Summarizing Sentences (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher provides 3-4 short paragraphs, each describing a fashion trend (past, present, or future) from the lesson's materials. Students, individually, must summarize each paragraph into a single sentence, using the target grammar structures. For example, "Original: People were really into bright colors and bold patterns in the 80s, but now many prefer a minimalist style. Summarized: In the 80s, people <em>were wearing</em> bright colors, but today, minimalist <em>style is in</em>."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Explain-Your-Trend (Semi-controlled):</b> In pairs, students receive a card with a sustainable fashion term (e.g., "upcycling," "vegan leather," "circular design"). They must explain the term to their partner using simple language and one example, without saying the word itself. Their partner guesses the term. They practice using phrases like, "This means..." or "It's about..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Group Consensus (Pair Interaction/Peer Check):</b> Groups of three receive three different short predictions about future fashion. Each student reads one prediction silently. Then, they collectively (without looking at each other's texts) must agree on the top two most important predictions and create a single, clear summary for each, using future simple. They must guide the discussion if there are disagreements, using phrases like, "Can we agree that X is more important?" or "How can we summarize this more effectively?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Sustainable Fashion".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative refinement and practice presentation of the "Fashion Prediction Infographic." Students work in their established project groups.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students retrieve their partially developed "Fashion Prediction Infographic" (from previous lessons or initial brainstorming).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In groups (3-4), students spend 5 minutes reviewing their infographic content. Each member then takes turns explaining one specific prediction or concept on their infographic to their group members, acting as a mediator. They must summarize the key points clearly, explain any complex fashion terms using simple language and (if available) visuals (e.g., showing a picture of an eco-friendly fabric sample). Example: "This section predicts that 'upcycling' will be a major trend. It means taking old clothes and making new ones, which will reduce waste."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The rest of the group listens, asks clarifying questions, and provides feedback on clarity and conciseness, guiding the speaker to improve their explanation. They also practice resolving any minor disagreements on wording or presentation using phrases like, "I think we should simplify this point, what do you think?", or "Can we choose one strong image to represent 'vintage style' effectively on our infographic?" This simulates resolving group conflicts in a respectful, mediated manner.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The groups refine their infographic, ensuring all sections are clearly explainable and summarized, preparing it for a final assessment/presentation.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on mediation skills, supported by a peer feedback sheet for the infographic explanation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group presents their "Fashion Prediction Infographic" to another group or the whole class (depending on time). Each student must explain and summarize at least one key prediction or concept from their infographic (e.g., "sustainable materials," "vintage revival," "technology in fashion"). They must use clear language, visuals, and incorporate future simple tense. They will also respond to questions from peers, demonstrating their ability to elaborate and clarify information effectively. Example: "Our infographic <em>predicts</em> that fashion <em>will adopt</em> more circular economy models. This means clothing <em>will be designed</em> to be recycled."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and conciseness when summarizing points from the infographic.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective explanation of fashion terms using simple language and visuals.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (<em>sustainable, eco-friendly, vintage, evolve, predict, materials</em>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of future simple for predictions, and past continuous/present simple for comparisons if used.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, especially of multisyllabic words like "sustainability" and "eco-friendly."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to resolve minor disagreements or guide group discussion effectively (observed during group work and presentation).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one piece of information about future fashion that you successfully explained to your peers today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did you help your group members understand a complex fashion term, or resolve a disagreement while creating your infographic?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did the process of summarizing and explaining information for your infographic help you become a better communicator about sustainable fashion?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific strategies did you use to clearly explain complex fashion terms, or to successfully resolve disagreements within your group during this project?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students effectively summarize and explain sustainable fashion concepts to their peers, utilizing the target grammar (future simple, past continuous/present simple) and vocabulary?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How well did students demonstrate the ability to resolve conflicts and guide discussions within their groups during the infographic project and presentation? Were specific mediation prompts helpful?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments are needed for future mediation tasks, perhaps providing more explicit language frames for summarizing or conflict resolution, or more varied texts for mediation?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the theme "What Is Sustainable Fashion?" and the associated 21st Century Skills Project. The next lesson will introduce a new theme and set of linguistic objectives, building on communication and critical thinking skills developed during this unit.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>